You can't get them once you're past XL1? You're kidding right?

Running the true protection racket as you've described is a suicide run.
The modified protection racket is a much better play and it rarely gets
finished at XL1. A quick recap of its elements:

1. XL1 is not a requirement. HP are a useful buffer. I generally shoot
for the mid to high 20s, which most often is XL2, or maybe even XL3. I
gain HP in 3 ways during the racket:

A. Praying. A helpful prayer when HP are below 6 will add to max HP as
long as current max HP do not exceed XL*5 + 10 HP (i.e. 15 for XL1, 20
for XL2, etc.) prayer restores all lost HP and bumps your max up to 5
extra HP. Can be rinsed and repeated if still below maximum after first
bump.

B. moving up an XL.

C. Quaffing healing potions. Gets a small bump.

Usually I'll do (A) until I exceed the max HP level for prayer. Then
I'll go ahead and move up an XL. If below the XL2 level repeat (A) again
at XL2 until above it. Finally I'll quaff a healing potion or
two sometimes. Gets you to max HP in the mid 20s with no problem.

2. Buff the pet. Get em large. Acquire another one if you get a chance
and get them large too. never enter the dungeon without a large pet.
Keep them healthy by zapping with healing spell if you have it.

3. Clean out shops. All shops all the time. Usually I'll scan the main
dungeon until I find the oracle. I locate every shop in the main dungeon
and clean it out. I usually use credit cloning simply because it's
convenient. But even if credit cloning is removed in the next edition, I
doubt the DevTeam will remove pet stealing altogether. Cleaning out
shops gives you a ton of loot to work with and a ton of money. Clean out
out all the shops in MineTown too. You can easily generate $10000 to
spend simply by systematically cleaning out every shop in the upper
dungeon and MineTown.

4. Armor up. All armor is fair game as long as it's not cursed. Even if
your spell failure rate is 100% put on the banded, mithril, shield, etc.
Make yourself as hard to hit as possible. Make sure to use the pet (or
even better a found altar) to BUC test each piece.

5. Find an engraving tool. Check even gem to find a hard one. Engrave
with wands (with the pet around just in case it's a create monster) to
see if a good engraving wand (fire, lightning, digging) pops up.
Personally I'm not a Elbereth user during the run. But it's handy in a
tight spot to know that you have a tool that'll get it on the ground
when you need it.

While the true protection racket may be entertaining, it's a whole lot
of work. The modified racket simply limits XL's, not halts them
completely. There's a vast difference in the price at XL6 or XL7 (along
with a completely different monster set) than at XL2 or XL3.


